<title>BELGIUM: Belgian CCB's H1 profit jumps 41.3 pct.</title>
<headline>Belgian CCB's H1 profit jumps 41.3 pct.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-09-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>First-half 1996 earnings</p>
<p>(in billions Belgian francs unless stated)</p>
<p>    Group net (excludes minorities)	   5.41 vs	3.83</p>
<p>    Interest income				20.67 vs     19.66</p>
<p>    Other banking income			 11.13 vs	8.51</p>
<p>    Total banking income			 31.80 vs     28.16</p>
<p>    Cons operating costs			 17.56 vs     16.76</p>
<p>    Provisions, write-downs, depreciation   5.25 vs	5.58</p>
<p>    Cons pretax profit (up 54.6 pct)	  8.99 vs	5.82</p>
<p>    Cons net profit				 6.20 vs	4.44</p>
<p>    Balance sheet assets		    3,727    vs  3,332</p>
<p>    Customer deposits (incl. bonds)     2,665    vs  2,574</p>
<p>    Own funds					73.8  vs     63.7</p>
<p>    Subordinated debt			    52.5  vs     44.2</p>
<p>    Risk asset ratio (in percentage)	 13.20 vs     n/a</p>
<p>    Return on equity			     15.11 vs    10.09</p>
<p>NOTE - CCB chief executive officer Francois Narmon told a news conference on Wednesday the 2.6 billion franc increase in other banking income came from a 1.5 billion franc rise in capital gains on the bank's investment portfolio and higher profits from financial services.</p>
<p>He said the 300 million franc fall in provisions was mainly due to lower provisions at CCB's 52-percent owned Luxembourg unit Banque Internationale a Luxembourg (BIL).</p>
<p>NOTE - Credit Communal de Belgique SA/Gemeentekrediet van Belgie NV is Belgium's second-largest bank. Part of its capital is to be bourse-listed in November under a planned alliance with French bank Credit Local de France.</p>
<p>-- Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6810, Fax +32 2 230 7710</p>
</text>
